WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.590
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and today
I'm going to talk about pronunciation.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.250
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.900
on the bell for notification.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.830
And for those of you who listen
on Apple Podcasts, please

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.690
feel free to leave a review.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.220
We greatly appreciate it.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.919
So pronunciation, the reason I want to
talk about pronunciation is actually

00:00:22.919 --> 00:00:24.029
two things that happened today.

00:00:24.089 --> 00:00:26.880
First of all, I had, we had
house guests, my wife and I.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.475
These are very good friends,
we knew them from Japan, uh, he

00:00:32.475 --> 00:00:35.325
was a consul general in Osaka.

00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:39.615
Uh, he was a language student of Japanese
way back when I knew him in the early

00:00:39.615 --> 00:00:42.165
seventies, he married a Japanese girl.

00:00:42.705 --> 00:00:47.415
And so, you know, we've seen them from
time to time over the last 50 years.

00:00:48.045 --> 00:00:48.775
They live in Kelowna.

00:00:49.275 --> 00:00:52.695
They came by on their way to Whistler
and they overnighted with us and

00:00:52.695 --> 00:00:58.214
we have, of course, we just talked
forever, went for walks and, uh, we were

00:00:58.214 --> 00:01:00.754
talking about wine and he referred...

00:01:00.775 --> 00:01:06.145
and he speaks French and he said,
uh, Sauvignon Blanc, Sauvignon.

00:01:06.164 --> 00:01:09.075
You know, I've heard
people say Sauvignon Blanc.

00:01:09.255 --> 00:01:10.865
He didn't, at least he didn't say Blanc.

00:01:11.685 --> 00:01:15.645
And I thought to myself, he knows, I
mean, we have the sound in English.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:18.645
It's not Sauvignon it's Sauvignon.

00:01:18.705 --> 00:01:22.995
So we have the word, we have
that "o" sound in English.

00:01:23.025 --> 00:01:25.425
Sauvignon, Sauvignon Blanc.

00:01:26.805 --> 00:01:31.635
But because in English were a bit
sloppy in how we pronounce things.

00:01:31.895 --> 00:01:34.935
Now, I'm sure, but I know he's a stickler.

00:01:34.955 --> 00:01:39.425
Like I think even in speaking French,
he would say Sauvignon, it's Sauvignon.

00:01:39.555 --> 00:01:43.395
So there's a certain lack
of attention to the sounds.

00:01:43.395 --> 00:01:44.625
You hear this all the time.

00:01:44.625 --> 00:01:45.105
And I think...

00:01:45.165 --> 00:01:50.025
I'm going to give examples in other
languages where our English habits, if

00:01:50.025 --> 00:01:55.470
we're English speakers, tend to dominate
our efforts to speak the foreign language.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.639
Uh, now the other thing that was
brought to my attention, and this is

00:01:59.639 --> 00:02:04.440
a, I think it was the person's name
was maybe Mike Murphy commenting here,

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:11.370
uh, on my, uh, one of my YouTube videos
about noticing that, uh, and he's been

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:17.174
teaching in China for a long time, and
the, even with all kinds of work with

00:02:17.174 --> 00:02:23.415
the international phonetic alphabet
IPA, which I find quite unhelpful, but

00:02:23.415 --> 00:02:25.095
people don't notice certain things.

00:02:25.905 --> 00:02:31.245
And so he noticed though that a lot
of Chinese people can't pronounce

00:02:31.245 --> 00:02:33.454
the word "usually" they say...

00:02:34.444 --> 00:02:37.855
and absolutely I've heard so
many Chinese people say...

00:02:37.885 --> 00:02:40.424
So I'm thinking about those two things.

00:02:41.445 --> 00:02:44.485
So in English, we're a
bit lazy with our vowels.

00:02:44.565 --> 00:02:50.115
So the vowelss, we don't say,
we don't clearly say Sauvignon.

00:02:50.235 --> 00:02:54.405
Uh, and that's how English
is, you know, we just kind of

00:02:54.405 --> 00:02:55.605
slur the vowels a little bit.

00:02:55.605 --> 00:03:00.945
We don't give the vowels the full value,
but there are languages where they do like

00:03:00.945 --> 00:03:04.005
French or Spanish or Japanese, full vowel.

00:03:04.755 --> 00:03:11.025
In Russian and in Portuguese, some
of the vows kind of fall away,

00:03:11.055 --> 00:03:15.085
but in, in many languages, they
want the full value for the vowel.

00:03:15.435 --> 00:03:18.075
That's not to say that
they don't also slur.

00:03:18.105 --> 00:03:23.905
So in French, you know, we want
to have a clear, you know...

00:03:24.455 --> 00:03:28.695
but people then end up saying...so
that they don't give it full value.

00:03:28.695 --> 00:03:29.805
And, you know, instead of saying...

00:03:31.135 --> 00:03:31.465
they say...

00:03:32.325 --> 00:03:37.275
So all of these things happen, but we
have to start by being able to notice

00:03:37.635 --> 00:03:42.945
the clear, clean, correct pronunciation,
and we have to be able to do it.

00:03:43.365 --> 00:03:48.165
And only once we have that correct
pronunciation, the ability to hear

00:03:48.165 --> 00:03:55.485
it and to reproduce that clear proper
sound for that vowel in that language

00:03:55.725 --> 00:03:57.835
only then can we afford to get sloppy.

00:03:58.695 --> 00:04:03.225
So, and going back to the Chinese example,
of course, if you've ever been to Beijing

00:04:03.225 --> 00:04:07.515
and taken a taxi, you can, I mean, even as
someone who speaks Chinese, it's hard to

00:04:07.515 --> 00:04:10.904
follow them because it's...it's a very...

00:04:10.904 --> 00:04:13.334
type of, if they're from Beijing.

00:04:13.935 --> 00:04:17.445
Uh, and so maybe that's what carries
over into the English instead of

00:04:17.445 --> 00:04:22.980
going "usually", you know, if you
really thought if that Chinese speaker

00:04:23.219 --> 00:04:24.719
would, would sort of look at it.

00:04:24.719 --> 00:04:27.060
And of course the problem in
English is that the words aren't

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.500
written the way they're pronounced.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.150
So it's not...

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.760
there's not a soft...

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.219
sounded like in French, you know, where
you would have a J, which has a...

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:42.600
sound usually has that same sound,
but it's written S U "usually".

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:48.795
And of course, in most words, S U
doesn't have that, uh, you know, S value.

00:04:48.835 --> 00:04:54.555
We say Supreme, Supreme,
suffer, uh, English is tough.

00:04:54.795 --> 00:04:55.635
It ain't, it's just tough.

00:04:55.815 --> 00:05:01.055
But I think that Chinese speakers and
there's so many of them who say...

00:05:01.315 --> 00:05:05.265
they have to sit down and
look at that word and practice

00:05:05.265 --> 00:05:07.545
saying that word correctly.

00:05:07.875 --> 00:05:12.885
And then eventually they'll be able to
incorporate that word into a sentence that

00:05:12.885 --> 00:05:14.805
they speak, you know, at normal speed.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.830
And I remember that, you know, now I've
learned lots of languages, but French

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:26.070
and, uh, and Chinese were my first and I
actually worked hard at the pronunciation.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.530
I remember the Chinese sound...

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:30.810
like

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:32.750
...
we don't have that in English.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:36.050
So I had to work at it and I
would pronounce Chinese and

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:37.180
I particularly remember...

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:45.635
And to the point where the muscles here
in my, in my jaw were kind of sore.

00:05:46.055 --> 00:05:50.705
Uh, I also remember trying very hard to
improve my French pronunciation because

00:05:51.185 --> 00:05:56.250
even though we say like "pronunciation",
You really, if you're an English speaker,

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:01.020
you have to work hard to force yourself
to say "pronunciation", because the

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:06.180
tendency from English, which, which is
what our brain is set up for is to say

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:11.849
"pronunciation" it's not pronounced
"pronunciation" it's "pronunciation",

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.740
and it's, it takes an extra bit of
effort to give it that full value.

00:06:17.745 --> 00:06:22.785
And once you have the full value, you
can then "pronunciation" you can kind

00:06:22.785 --> 00:06:26.445
of slur over it a bit, but if you
don't have that essential, you know,

00:06:26.445 --> 00:06:30.645
that core ability to make those sounds
correctly, then you're going to end

00:06:30.645 --> 00:06:34.695
up with the equivalent of "usually"
that the Chinese use for usually.

00:06:35.145 --> 00:06:37.035
And this is true in all languages.

00:06:37.935 --> 00:06:42.485
I mean, Spanish also has pure vowels
and they're not dipthongs, so it's

00:06:42.485 --> 00:06:47.280
not "bueno" an English speaker will
say "bueno", "no bueno" or whatever.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:48.200
It's "bueno".

00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:48.880
"bueno".

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.670
We have to focus in on that.

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:54.480
And I don't think the, uh,
international phonetic alphabet helps.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:59.310
I think you have to listen very carefully
and then repeat very deliberately.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:05.580
And, uh, you know, in a way, if you do
the mini stories at LingQ you get sentence

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:11.130
by sentence and you can hear the natural
uh, you know, voice read those sentences.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:14.910
So you can, you can listen to
the sound, text to speech for

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:18.960
one word, but you can also hear a
sentence and then try to imitate it.

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:23.250
And when you try to imitate it,
really work the jaw muscles so that

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.590
you're giving it the full value.

00:07:26.325 --> 00:07:29.164
If in the case of, uh, if
it's Chinese, then it's...

00:07:29.344 --> 00:07:30.145
The real...

00:07:30.314 --> 00:07:31.094
is coming in.

00:07:31.304 --> 00:07:36.875
If it's French...if it's, uh, you
know, "bueno", "bueno" cut that "a",

00:07:36.875 --> 00:07:38.955
it's not a "bueno" it's a "bueno".

00:07:39.955 --> 00:07:42.125
And we have to hear those things.

00:07:42.335 --> 00:07:46.414
We have to develop the ability to hear
the difference between how we sound

00:07:46.414 --> 00:07:50.435
and how the native speaker sounds and
focus in on some of these key sounds.

00:07:50.435 --> 00:07:55.345
So you build up a base, the ability to
make these sounds and it's difficult

00:07:55.345 --> 00:07:58.615
at first, you might make it once, but
then within a sentence, you'll lose it.

00:07:58.915 --> 00:08:03.865
I'll always remember in Montreal, I
used to hitchhike to McGill University

00:08:03.865 --> 00:08:08.095
because you know, it was a long bus
ride and there was another fellow

00:08:08.095 --> 00:08:12.505
hitchhiking and he was an immigrant
from Southern Italy and we got friendly.

00:08:12.505 --> 00:08:16.435
In fact, we ended up looking for work
on construction sites in Montreal

00:08:16.435 --> 00:08:19.795
together, and his English was
terrible and I was trying to help him.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:23.010
And, uh, so he couldn't say small.

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.650
He would always say "small".

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:30.240
And it was frustrating, I said
saying no, no, no, no small.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:31.230
Uh, okay.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:34.710
So I would say go small.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:41.020
And he went "small" for whatever reason
he was preprogrammed to say "small".

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.610
Maybe he heard it from other
people from Southern Italy.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:45.210
I don't know.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:46.290
I don't know.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.010
In the end we worked construction
for a couple of months.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:52.410
And then I went down to the dock
and I was able to hitchhike on a

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:54.959
boat going across to Europe so I
worked on a boat going across to

00:08:54.959 --> 00:08:56.480
Europe and I haven't seen him since.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.390
I don't know if he still says "small".

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:00.220
Sounds like the...

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:03.180
in French and in other languages
where they have an...sound,

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:04.980
that takes a lot of work.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.610
And you know, that traditional
sort of suggestion is to go...

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.580
put your mouth in sort
of the shape to go...

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:18.730
and then just try and say E so if
you go...you'll end up with an...

00:09:19.089 --> 00:09:23.160
even if you're able to produce
the...to get to the point where you can

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:29.000
produce it on the fly in a phrase or a
sentence, it takes a lot of practice.

00:09:29.445 --> 00:09:33.045
Uh, so you got to notice it, you gotta
practice it individually and then you

00:09:33.045 --> 00:09:35.355
want to get into doing it in sentences.

00:09:35.745 --> 00:09:40.845
So my suggestion on pronunciation is
begin by really focusing, and so if you're

00:09:40.845 --> 00:09:45.195
a Chinese speaker and you say you were
really get ahold of that word, look at it,

00:09:45.225 --> 00:09:53.265
break it down, say u-su-a-lly, usually,
usually, usually usually get to where you

00:09:53.265 --> 00:09:58.604
can say the word and then usually I like
to go, you know, and usually when I study

00:09:58.604 --> 00:09:59.984
languages, I do this, that or the other.

00:10:00.165 --> 00:10:01.814
So throw it into a sentence.

00:10:01.935 --> 00:10:07.755
Now, second suggestion is to pronounce
well there's two things we have to

00:10:07.755 --> 00:10:10.645
do: we got to hear it and we got
to be able to produce the sound.

00:10:10.665 --> 00:10:13.245
We don't have to get a hundred
percent, but more or less.

00:10:13.964 --> 00:10:18.925
The second thing is we don't
want to resist that sound.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.350
You know, there are, we don't
want to resist anything that

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:23.490
has to do with that language.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:32.460
So, you know, Swedish...and if
you uh feel self-conscious about

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:34.260
doing that, then you won't do it.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:37.020
So you've got to not feel self-conscious.

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:42.010
And in that regard, I find that if you
can get a hold of, in that regard our

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:46.500
mini stories at LingQ are great because
they're kind of narrated without feeling.

00:10:47.535 --> 00:10:50.954
I enjoyed when I was learning
Mandarin, I enjoyed the...

00:10:51.474 --> 00:10:54.165
dialogues because there was a
lot of feeling in those, these

00:10:54.224 --> 00:10:55.905
comedians telling a story.

00:10:56.714 --> 00:11:00.165
And, uh, I think the same when we
learn languages, sometimes if we can

00:11:00.165 --> 00:11:04.064
get ahold of something that's spoken
with feeling, that we connect at an

00:11:04.064 --> 00:11:08.775
emotional level, and sometimes we can
have fun with it in different languages.

00:11:08.775 --> 00:11:09.675
You know, we can say...

00:11:12.939 --> 00:11:17.459
just sort of get into the...

00:11:17.739 --> 00:11:24.540
you get into sort of almost like a
situation where emotion is involved and

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:29.189
so you, you play at the language and
so that way you're connecting, you're

00:11:29.189 --> 00:11:30.589
creating some emotional connection.

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:33.360
The phrasiology and the pronunciation.

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.530
So I think all of those
things are important.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:41.819
It begins by getting a grasp on how
it's actually pronounced, uh, you

00:11:41.819 --> 00:11:45.300
know, making the effort to pronounce
correctly, which is more effort.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:49.630
It's an effort to say"pronunciation"
"Sauvignon Blanc".

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:53.100
You gotta work at it until
eventually it becomes natural.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:59.190
And then get a hold of something
that you can emotionally connect to.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:02.295
And then imitate when people
speak with some emotion.

00:12:03.135 --> 00:12:07.785
In most situations, you won't be,
you know, kind of wrapped up in that.

00:12:08.385 --> 00:12:10.635
And, uh, but it, again, it helps.

00:12:10.665 --> 00:12:14.055
And, and I think for people having
learned as many languages as I have,

00:12:14.475 --> 00:12:20.325
and I have to admit that the first few
languages I worked hard on pronunciation.

00:12:20.685 --> 00:12:24.795
And then I think that introduces a
level of flexibility in the brain.

00:12:25.485 --> 00:12:30.075
So the subsequent languages, first of
all, there's a greater likelihood that

00:12:30.075 --> 00:12:34.725
there will be sounds in there that we have
already noticed and been able to produce.

00:12:35.925 --> 00:12:39.315
We're more confident, we're
less self-conscious and

00:12:39.315 --> 00:12:40.545
so things become easier.

00:12:40.695 --> 00:12:43.935
But the first few languages, I
think one has to make a special

00:12:43.935 --> 00:12:47.865
effort to get, you know, a certain
minimum level of pronunciation.

00:12:47.865 --> 00:12:52.935
It doesn't have to be perfect, but, um,
yeah, when I hear Sauvignon Blanc, if

00:12:52.935 --> 00:12:56.025
the person is trying to speak French,,
to me that's just not good enough.

00:12:56.324 --> 00:12:57.885
Anyway, there you have it.

00:12:58.095 --> 00:13:00.245
Enjoy your Sauvignon Blanc.

00:13:01.005 --> 00:13:01.495
Bye for now.

